stView\n\nstruct AlarmListView: View {\n    let alarms: [Alarm]\n\n    var body: some View {\n        if alarms.isEmpty {\n            emptyState\n        } else {\n            ScrollView {\n                LazyVStack(spacing: 12) {\n                    ForEach(alarms) { alarm in\n                        AlarmCard(alarm: alarm)\n                    }\n                }\n                .padding(.horizontal, 20)\n                .padding(.bottom, 100)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Empty state: message + ghost sample cards so the layout reads naturally\n    private var emptyState: some View {\n        ScrollView {\n            VStack(spacing: 16) {\n                VStack(spacing: 8) {\n                    Text(\"🌿\")\n                        .font(.system(size: 56))\n                    Text(\"還沒有鬧鐘\")\n                        .font(GhibliFonts.title(22))\n                        .foregroundStyle(GhibliColors.nightIndigo)\n                    Text(\"點右下角的 + 來新增第一個吧！\")\n                        .font(GhibliFonts.caption())\n                        .foregroundStyle(GhibliColors.totoroGray)\n                }\n                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                .padding(.top, 24)\n\n                LazyVStack(spacing: 12) {\n                    ForEach(sampleAlarmData) { data in\n                        SampleAlarmCard(data: data)\n                    }\n                }\n                .padding(.horizontal, 20)\n                .opacity(0.4)\n            }\n            .padding(.bottom, 100)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// MARK: - AlarmCard (interactive, wrapped in WatercolorCard)\n\nprivate struct AlarmCard: View {\n    @Bindable var alarm: Alarm\n    @State private var showingRecording = false\n\n    var body: some View {\n        WatercolorCard {","numLines":80,"startLine":1,"totalLines":175}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01RE1xxZSEwVyRG2ZvFkzEFi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a solid picture. Let me review what I know:\n\n**Day 10 actual milestone (from spec)**: \n- Spec says Week 2, Day 10-11: `SpeechRecognizer`, console prints recognized text\n- But by now (Days 1-9), A has already delivered SpeechRecognizer (Day 5), AlarmRingView full flow (Day 4-5), ParentalGateView (Day 6), UNUserNotificationCenterDelegate (Day 7), and much more. The spec is running far ahead.\n- Day 9's brief (which is what Day 10 was executing) called for:\n  1. Voice fallback button in AlarmRingView when recognition fails three times\n  2. ConfettiSwiftUI in RewardView\n  3. Fixing the `sampleAlarms` preview model in AlarmListView\n  4. iPad landscape guard in HomeView\n\n**What A actually delivered on Day 10**: The voice fallback button is working—it shows a large tap button after three failed recognitions—and ConfettiSwiftUI has been integrated into the project and wired up in RewardView.\n\nThe struct-based preview data in AlarmListView is fixed, and the iPad landscape layout now properly switches to a compact view when in landscape orientation. Two new voice fallback tests were added, bringing the total to 38.\n\nNow I'm checking C's concern about the fallback button integration—specifically whether the `onFailure` callback is properly wired between `SpeechRecognizer` and `AlarmRingView`. Looking at the SpeechRecognizer implementation, the `startListening` method does support an optional `onFailure` callback that fires when recognition errors occur and the listener stops. In AlarmRingView, the speech cycle is calling this with the proper callback...\n\nBut there's a critical gap: `onFailure` only triggers on actual recognition errors, not when the user simply says nothing or the recognizer returns no keyword match. If the speech task completes without finding a match and without