cognizer error, not when the child just says random words or stays silent—so in normal conditions where the recognizer works fine, the failure count might never increment at all, meaning the fallback button could be unreachable. I'm realizing there's a potential threading issue here—the `SpeechRecognizer` is marked `@MainActor`, but the closure passed to `recognitionTask` runs on SFSpeechRecognizer's background queue, and from there it's calling `self.stop()` and the `onFailure` callback, which could violate main-actor isolation. The `onFailure` callback only triggers on recognizer errors, not when the child fails to say the keyword within the timeout window — so the fallback button won't appear in the typical case where the child simply can't produce the phrase, only for actual system failures. There's also a minor issue where `handleRecognitionFailure()` reassigns `speechTask` without canceling the previous one first, though in practice this doesn't cause problems since the old task completes before the failure callback fires. The recognition task callback runs on a background queue and calls `@MainActor`-isolated methods, which is a data race concern in theory though not a practical crash issue with Swift's current implementation.\n\nLooking at the session history, the team handled the token limit well — the first two sessions completed their work and A verified the missing pbxproj piece. B caught the missing `PBXFrameworksBuildPhase`, and C applied the three-step fix correctly before committing. C's concerns about the iPad landscape size class issue are valid.\n\nAgainst the spec, the SpeechRecognizer milestone was already delivered on Day 5, well ahead of the Day 10-11 target. The voice fallback requirement is structurally there but has the gap I mentioned about `onFailure` not covering timeout cases. ConfettiSwiftUI and the AlarmListView cleanup are both spec-aligned, and the iPad landscape handling still needs attention.\n\nI'm reconsidering the \"at_risk\" r